WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.587
This briefing comes from Book 06 of
the Federal Modernization Workbook:

2
00:00:04.587 --> 00:00:09.350
the SQL Server estate's move from
Windows Authentication to cloud identity.

3
00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:12.945
Every identity program
eventually reaches the database,

4
00:00:12.945 --> 00:00:17.887
and the database is where it finds the
most Windows authentication still in use.

5
00:00:17.887 --> 00:00:22.222
I'll take it through why that is, what
replaces it, who owns the work, and how

6
00:00:22.222 --> 00:00:27.280
to do it, from the estate inventory to a
closure condition you can keep proving.

7
00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:31.555
Why is the database where
legacy authentication hides?

8
00:00:31.555 --> 00:00:34.880
Because SQL Server trusts
whatever Windows tells it.

9
00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.967
A connection reaches the
engine in one of four ways.

10
00:00:37.967 --> 00:00:41.180
Kerberos, when the service
principal name is right.

11
00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:45.305
NTLM, silently, when the SPN is missing.

12
00:00:45.305 --> 00:00:48.905
A SQL login, where the engine
checks a password itself.

13
00:00:48.905 --> 00:00:51.306
Or Microsoft Entra authentication,

14
00:00:51.306 --> 00:00:54.668
which is the only one
Conditional Access ever sees.

15
00:00:54.668 --> 00:00:57.306
Three facts make the
inheritance hard to see.

16
00:00:57.306 --> 00:01:00.625
Windows Authentication can't
be turned off in SQL Server,

17
00:01:00.625 --> 00:01:04.580
because the engine delegates it to
the Windows security subsystem.

18
00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:08.808
Which protocol a connection
used isn't written to any log;

19
00:01:08.808 --> 00:01:13.755
it shows only in a dynamic management
view, sys.dm_exec_connections.

20
00:01:13.755 --> 00:01:17.835
And local connections use NTLM
even when the SPN is right,

21
00:01:17.835 --> 00:01:23.218
while an SPN registered on the wrong
account makes the login fail outright.

22
00:01:23.218 --> 00:01:25.348
The book is careful about one thing:

23
00:01:25.348 --> 00:01:29.829
Windows Authentication was good
engineering for a domain-joined estate.

24
00:01:29.829 --> 00:01:33.726
The case for replacing it rests
on that world no longer existing,

25
00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:35.700
not on calling it a mistake.

26
00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.738
Three drivers make the inheritance end.

27
00:01:38.738 --> 00:01:41.912
First, NTLM elimination.

28
00:01:41.912 --> 00:01:46.592
Microsoft deprecated all versions
of NTLM in June 2024 and

29
00:01:46.592 --> 00:01:51.175
plans to disable it by default
in a future Windows release.

30
00:01:51.175 --> 00:01:57.625
The program sets its own backstop, April
1, 2027, for the domain-wide block.

31
00:01:57.625 --> 00:02:01.100
Second, Zero Trust in
the authentication path.

32
00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:06.806
OMB M-22-09 puts phishing-resistant
MFA and the Entra identity plane

33
00:02:06.806 --> 00:02:11.575
in front of human access, and
Windows Authentication bypasses both.

34
00:02:11.575 --> 00:02:13.950
Third, service-account credentials.

35
00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:17.990
The directory modernization in the
Federal Modernization Workbook's

36
00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:21.962
Book 02 ends the domain-joined world
those service accounts depend on.

37
00:02:21.962 --> 00:02:24.662
The book is precise about 2027.

38
00:02:24.662 --> 00:02:27.118
There's no external NTLM deadline;

39
00:02:27.118 --> 00:02:30.801
Microsoft hasn't dated the
disable-by-default step.

40
00:02:30.801 --> 00:02:34.507
The dates that are real are support dates:

41
00:02:34.507 --> 00:02:38.759
SQL Server 2016 went out
of support in July 2026,

42
00:02:38.759 --> 00:02:44.974
Windows Server 2016 follows in
January 2027, the last SQL Server 2014

43
00:02:44.974 --> 00:02:48.681
extended-security year ends in July 2027,

44
00:02:48.681 --> 00:02:53.588
and SQL Server 2017 goes out
of support in October 2027.

45
00:02:53.588 --> 00:02:58.530
SQL Server 2014 and 2017 can't
use Entra authentication at all,

46
00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:03.720
so they reach the closed state
only by upgrade or retirement.

47
00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:06.895
What changes starts
with the estate itself.

48
00:03:06.895 --> 00:03:11.382
Before touching authentication, every
instance gets one of three verdicts.

49
00:03:11.382 --> 00:03:16.722
Retain: it's supported or can be upgraded
in place to SQL Server 2022 or later,

50
00:03:16.722 --> 00:03:21.458
it carries load-bearing work, and
nothing argues for collapsing it.

51
00:03:21.458 --> 00:03:27.083
Consolidate: its workload moves into a target
platform and the old instance retires.

52
00:03:27.083 --> 00:03:31.908
Retire: there's no live workload, so
it's decommissioned instead of migrated.

53
00:03:31.908 --> 00:03:34.533
The inventory has to
cover the whole forest.

54
00:03:34.533 --> 00:03:39.095
The setspn tool needs its -F switch
to search beyond a single domain.

55
00:03:39.095 --> 00:03:42.446
Usage, versions and
dependencies are read together,

56
00:03:42.446 --> 00:03:47.354
and the book warns that index-usage
counters reset when SQL Server restarts,

57
00:03:47.354 --> 00:03:49.770
so they can't prove disuse on their own.

58
00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:55.187
And the assessment uses the migration
component in SQL Server Management Studio,

59
00:03:55.187 --> 00:03:59.807
because Microsoft retired the Data
Migration Assistant in July 2025.

60
00:03:59.807 --> 00:04:01.808
This is the gate for the whole program.

61
00:04:01.808 --> 00:04:04.287
Migrating an instance
that should have been

62
00:04:04.287 --> 00:04:07.380
retired is the most expensive
mistake it can make.

63
00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:10.030
What replaces the domain join?

64
00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:13.443
Azure Arc gives each
instance an Entra identity.

65
00:04:13.443 --> 00:04:17.742
On SQL Server 2025, that's
the Arc managed identity.

66
00:04:17.742 --> 00:04:22.654
On SQL Server 2022, it's an app
registration with a certificate.

67
00:04:22.654 --> 00:04:26.692
The Azure extension for SQL Server
installs automatically when the

68
00:04:26.692 --> 00:04:30.517
host connects to Arc, and Entra
connections are always encrypted.

69
00:04:30.517 --> 00:04:33.818
The limits matter, and the
old documents missed several.

70
00:04:33.818 --> 00:04:38.906
Failover cluster instances can't use
Entra authentication on any version.

71
00:04:38.906 --> 00:04:43.835
Entra authentication through Arc isn't
available in the US Government Virginia

72
00:04:43.835 --> 00:04:48.250
region, so a GCC tenant uses a commercial
subscription in the same tenant;

73
00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:53.180
the old runbook's Azure Government setup
would never have produced Entra logins.

74
00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:56.443
FIDO2 isn't supported
for SQL Server on Arc.

75
00:04:56.443 --> 00:05:00.298
And granting the instance identity
its Graph permissions needs a

76
00:05:00.298 --> 00:05:04.155
Privileged Role Administrator, so
that step can't sit with the DBAs.

77
00:05:04.155 --> 00:05:07.730
Arc is the program's chosen
path, not the only one:

78
00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:13.505
Microsoft documents a manual setup for
SQL Server 2022 and later on Windows.

79
00:05:13.505 --> 00:05:18.292
Arc wins because it also carries
inventory, policy, updates and Defender

80
00:05:18.292 --> 00:05:23.040
for the host, which is the Federal
Modernization Workbook's Book 05.

81
00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:27.578
The logins themselves move to
Entra principals, in parallel.

82
00:05:27.578 --> 00:05:31.438
People become Entra users,
ideally granted through groups,

83
00:05:31.438 --> 00:05:35.377
and they get phishing-resistant
MFA and Conditional Access.

84
00:05:35.377 --> 00:05:40.195
Groups become Entra group logins, which
SQL Server records as type X, with

85
00:05:40.195 --> 00:05:43.166
membership driven by
HR attributes from the

86
00:05:43.166 --> 00:05:45.977
Federal Modernization Workbook's Book 03.

87
00:05:45.977 --> 00:05:49.666
Applications become service
principals and managed identities,

88
00:05:49.666 --> 00:05:54.091
and the book is clear that Conditional
Access doesn't cover managed identities.

89
00:05:54.091 --> 00:05:56.390
The migration is a parallel run.

90
00:05:56.390 --> 00:06:01.465
Create the Entra user beside the Windows
user and copy its roles and permissions.

91
00:06:01.465 --> 00:06:05.301
Keep both until the audit shows
no remaining Windows sessions,

92
00:06:05.301 --> 00:06:06.777
then disable the old one.

93
00:06:06.777 --> 00:06:12.364
Never convert in one step; and the old
runbook's shortcut, ALTER USER with LOGIN,

94
00:06:12.364 --> 00:06:16.815
only converts on Azure SQL Managed
Instance, not on SQL Server.

95
00:06:16.815 --> 00:06:21.713
One trap: the instance's Entra
administrator becomes sysadmin, and changing

96
00:06:21.713 --> 00:06:26.730
that administrator leaves the old
login as sysadmin until a DBA drops it.

97
00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:28.330
Who owns it?

98
00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:31.843
The book's new Chapter 13
separates deciding from doing.

99
00:06:31.843 --> 00:06:37.567
Deciding: the CIO and data leadership
decide retain, consolidate or retire.

100
00:06:37.567 --> 00:06:42.167
The CISO, the ISSO and the
authorizing official decide

101
00:06:42.167 --> 00:06:46.367
NTLM and SQL-login exceptions
and sign off closure.

102
00:06:46.367 --> 00:06:51.306
Application and data owners decide the
cutover window for each application.

103
00:06:51.306 --> 00:06:57.055
Doing: the data platform team runs logins,
roles, the audit and the parallel run.

104
00:06:57.055 --> 00:07:00.484
The identity team creates
Entra logins and groups

105
00:07:00.484 --> 00:07:03.342
and grants the Graph
consent the DBAs can't.

106
00:07:03.342 --> 00:07:05.905
The server team onboards hosts to Arc.

107
00:07:05.905 --> 00:07:09.779
Supporting: developers change
connection strings and drivers,

108
00:07:09.779 --> 00:07:12.922
the help desk handles sign-in
issues during cutover,

109
00:07:12.922 --> 00:07:16.430
and security operations watches
Defender for SQL alerts.

110
00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:18.305
And there are hidden administrators.

111
00:07:18.305 --> 00:07:23.452
Azure Contributor on an Arc server is an
indirect administrator of SQL Server on

112
00:07:23.452 --> 00:07:26.294
that host, and Owner or
Contributor on the SQL

113
00:07:26.294 --> 00:07:29.367
resource can switch SQL
authentication back on.

114
00:07:29.367 --> 00:07:33.150
Azure role assignments need
the same care as sysadmin.

115
00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:34.513
How.

116
00:07:34.513 --> 00:07:37.749
NTLM is eliminated in three phases.

117
00:07:37.749 --> 00:07:41.015
Phase A discovers: the
authentication audit,

118
00:07:41.015 --> 00:07:45.162
together with Windows NTLM
logging and auditing events.

119
00:07:45.162 --> 00:07:50.199
Phase B restricts: fix SPNs, convert
connections to Kerberos or Entra,

120
00:07:50.199 --> 00:07:52.850
and migrate the remaining patterns.

121
00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:57.851
Phase C eliminates: the
domain-wide Restrict NTLM block,

122
00:07:57.851 --> 00:08:01.112
on the program's April 2027 backstop.

123
00:08:01.112 --> 00:08:02.487
The details matter.

124
00:08:02.487 --> 00:08:08.904
LAN Manager authentication level five
refuses LM and NTLM version one but still

125
00:08:08.904 --> 00:08:12.352
accepts version two, so
it isn't an NTLM block;

126
00:08:12.352 --> 00:08:15.512
the block is the Restrict NTLM policies.

127
00:08:15.512 --> 00:08:19.982
Their exceptions are lists of server
names on clients and domain controllers,

128
00:08:19.982 --> 00:08:22.113
not the program's exception registry.

129
00:08:22.113 --> 00:08:27.916
And Microsoft's October 2026 change
blocks NTLM-version-one-derived single

130
00:08:27.916 --> 00:08:33.351
sign-on credentials for Wi-Fi and VPN;
it doesn't touch SQL Server traffic.

131
00:08:33.351 --> 00:08:36.087
The fastest exit is migration itself.

132
00:08:36.087 --> 00:08:41.222
An instance whose logins have moved to
Entra ID isn't affected by the block,

133
00:08:41.222 --> 00:08:45.620
so login migration and NTLM
elimination are the same work.

134
00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:47.834
With the logins moved,

135
00:08:47.834 --> 00:08:52.145
access is granted by attribute
and applications move by driver.

136
00:08:52.145 --> 00:08:57.037
Access control: dynamic Entra
groups, driven by HR attributes,

137
00:08:57.037 --> 00:09:01.258
grant database access, so no
DBA provisions it by hand.

138
00:09:01.258 --> 00:09:04.332
Administrative units
delegate group management,

139
00:09:04.332 --> 00:09:08.870
privileged database roles go through
PIM from the Federal Modernization

140
00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:12.895
Workbook's Book 04, and reviews cover
the groups, not just the logins.

141
00:09:12.895 --> 00:09:16.264
The application chain: the
book sorts SQL-consuming

142
00:09:16.264 --> 00:09:20.495
applications into four classes,
each with its own migration path.

143
00:09:20.495 --> 00:09:23.788
Connection strings name a
specific Entra authentication

144
00:09:23.788 --> 00:09:26.745
mode in production rather
than the catch-all default.

145
00:09:26.745 --> 00:09:32.600
Microsoft.Data.SqlClient, ODBC, OLE
DB and JDBC all support Entra modes,

146
00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:37.345
and the book has a driver table
with the minimum versions.

147
00:09:37.345 --> 00:09:41.983
Managed identities on Arc hosts get their
tokens from the local agent service.

148
00:09:41.983 --> 00:09:44.208
The last mile is the connection string.

149
00:09:44.208 --> 00:09:49.670
Every application that still says Integrated
Security keeps the Windows path alive,

150
00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:54.040
so the application inventory is
part of closure, not a follow-up.

151
00:09:54.790 --> 00:09:58.476
The book's last idea is that a
transformation doesn't complete;

152
00:09:58.476 --> 00:10:02.378
it achieves a posture, and a posture
is something you keep proving.

153
00:10:02.378 --> 00:10:06.207
The closed state has five
conditions that hold together:

154
00:10:06.207 --> 00:10:09.873
every in-scope instance on
Entra-only authentication;

155
00:10:09.873 --> 00:10:15.575
no SQL logins outside approved exceptions;
no NTLM sessions in the continuous audit,

156
00:10:15.575 --> 00:10:20.463
counting remote sessions, because
local ones always show NTLM; any drift,

157
00:10:20.463 --> 00:10:24.129
such as an administrator
switching back to mixed mode,

158
00:10:24.129 --> 00:10:28.935
detected and treated as a violation;
and evidence regenerated from SQL

159
00:10:28.935 --> 00:10:33.416
Server Audit and the auth_scheme
field, not assembled into a binder.

160
00:10:33.416 --> 00:10:35.652
One correction matters for assessors.

161
00:10:35.652 --> 00:10:40.823
Entra sign-in logs can't prove per-instance
access or the absence of NTLM,

162
00:10:40.823 --> 00:10:44.766
because every SQL resource
shares one token audience.

163
00:10:44.766 --> 00:10:47.265
The proof has to come
from the instance itself.

164
00:10:47.265 --> 00:10:49.465
And there's a certificate
layer underneath.

165
00:10:49.465 --> 00:10:52.952
SQL Server's TLS certificates
still come from Active

166
00:10:52.952 --> 00:10:56.202
Directory Certificate
Services in most estates.

167
00:10:56.202 --> 00:11:01.443
Microsoft Cloud PKI issues only to
Intune-managed devices, and Intune doesn't

168
00:11:01.443 --> 00:11:06.970
manage Windows Server, so the
server-certificate issuer is its own decision.

169
00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:08.945
So, four asks.

170
00:11:08.945 --> 00:11:13.086
Run the inventory and the gate:
a retain, consolidate or retire

171
00:11:13.086 --> 00:11:17.633
verdict for every instance before
any authentication work starts.

172
00:11:17.633 --> 00:11:22.752
Fund the Arc bridge: a commercial
subscription in the GCC tenant,

173
00:11:22.752 --> 00:11:26.133
with SQL Server 2022 or
later as the target.

174
00:11:26.133 --> 00:11:31.520
Plan the support dates: upgrade
or retire the 2014, 2016 and 2017

175
00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:37.944
instances on their real dates, because
they can't use Entra authentication.

176
00:11:37.944 --> 00:11:41.403
And decide the certificate
issuer: the Active Directory

177
00:11:41.403 --> 00:11:45.670
Certificate Services dependency for
server TLS needs its own answer.

178
00:11:45.670 --> 00:11:49.595
The database is where legacy
authentication hides longest.

179
00:11:49.595 --> 00:11:53.019
Bringing it into Entra ID is
what lets Conditional Access,

180
00:11:53.019 --> 00:11:58.083
phishing-resistant authentication and
continuous evidence finally reach the data.

181
00:11:58.083 --> 00:12:02.254
The Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 06 has the narrative

182
00:12:02.254 --> 00:12:05.796
and ten runbooks, with every
external claim footnoted.

183
00:12:05.796 --> 00:12:11.745
Book 05 covers Arc on servers, and Book
04 the access policy it plugs into.

184
00:12:11.745 --> 00:12:12.570
Thank you.
